    {
      "name": "methods",
      "text": "For the additional Gaussian interval diagnostics, central 90\\% and 95\\% intervals use standard-normal multipliers $z=1.645$ and $z=1.960$, respectively (rounded here; the archived calculations use full-precision quantiles). Prediction-interval coverage probability (PICP) is the proportion of observations within the interval, and mean prediction-interval width (MPIW) is the mean upper minus lower bound in mm\\,h$^{-1}$. We report the negative-lower-bound proportion at both levels, together with diagnostic coverage for the observed wet ($y\\geq0.1$~mm\\,h$^{-1}$) and upper-tail ($y\\geq0.574048$~mm\\,h$^{-1}$, using training Q95) subsets. These diagnostics use the separate q14 five-seed Gaussian reruns summarised in Supplementary Table~\\ref{supp-stab:r2minor2_intervals}.",
      "sha256": "8b185858572cde0df19b0019dda3685ffbcae504572c94a881fef57e0f6b943a"
    },
    {
      "name": "results",
      "text": "Supplementary Table~\\ref{supp-stab:r2minor2_intervals} reports both interval levels for the separate q14 five-seed Gaussian reruns on the same 3,939 test targets. Across the three models, mean all-sample coverage was 0.9456--0.9464 for the nominal 90\\% interval and 0.9560--0.9571 for the nominal 95\\% interval. The corresponding widths were 0.6526--0.6705 and 0.7776--0.7989~mm\\,h$^{-1}$. For the 854 wet targets, coverage was 0.7546--0.7571 and 0.8002--0.8042, respectively; for the 238 upper-tail targets, it was 0.3958--0.4101 and 0.4941--0.5042. At both nominal levels, every lower bound was negative and every uncovered observation exceeded the upper bound. Thus, near-nominal overall 95\\% coverage and above-nominal overall 90\\% coverage conceal marked wet and upper-tail undercoverage. These are descriptive conditional diagnostics, not evidence of a significant model ranking.",
      "sha256": "c7f2362d9e16d86c76a7bb7726de911bf987bfdea6f9357856195ea72285cf97"
    },
    {
      "name": "identity",
      "text": "These reruns are the A2 run set in Table~\\ref{tab:probabilistic_results}, rather than the complete original A1 run set. The A2 Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention and the B Gaussian have identical saved test targets and predictive means for each of the five seeds, with small differences in the saved standard-deviation parameters (at most $1.23\\times10^{-7}$~mm\\,h$^{-1}$). Their agreement therefore does not constitute independent prediction evidence, despite the separate run records.",
      "sha256": "a46628943eaada5674c94e19d496d8f9187eff7d34670057b0964bd05ebb32da"
    }
  ]
